  "interview": {
    "expected_score": 12,
    "expected_score_probability": 50,
    "full_score_probability": 1,
    "hardest_part": "Navigating the hotel interior without a map: correlating doorways, stairs and corridor turns from single screenshots, and noticing when a dark or cluttered room hides the actual exit, since progress is measured as distance along the nav-mesh route and any wrong turn costs many 0.5 s steps.",
    "likely_failure": "Getting stuck on geometry or wandering off-route in a tight corridor or doorway — pressing into a wall or a closed door, losing sight of the bots' direction, and then oscillating instead of progressing; a second likely failure is being downed in a horde choke point while reloading or while the view is pointed away from the attackers.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Three AI teammates who push forward and can revive or heal me, Easy difficulty with forgiving damage and plentiful supplies, generous 150-unit horizontal route tolerance so small deviations still count, and the fact that each new percent is banked permanently once touched, so even messy wandering along the route accumulates points.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Arrow-key-only turning with a fixed 0.5 s step makes fine aiming and cornering imprecise, no minimap or compass means heading must be inferred from visuals alone, the first-person view can be obstructed by darkness and smoke, hordes of common infected plus special infected can interrupt long movement sequences, and the pause-per-action cadence limits how quickly I can chain 'move forward' steps along long corridors."
  }
